 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** the official data says Perth electricity prices aren't rising unusually fast, so a big bill probably comes from something other than the price per unit.\n\n**Electricity prices, annual change in August 2026 (ABS CPI)**\n\n| City | Annual change |\n|---|---|\n| Perth | +2.4% |\n| Brisbane | +5.9% |\n| Sydney | +13.2% |\n| Melbourne | +16.8% |\n| Adelaide | +21.4% |\n| Australia | +13.2% |\n\n- Perth has the smallest electricity increase of the cities I checked. It has been about +2.4–2.5% every month from April to August 2026.\n- Overall inflation in Perth was 4.3% in the year to August 2026, against 4.0% nationally. So the general cost of living is rising faster than your electricity prices.\n\n**Why your bill might still feel huge**\n- **Rebates ending:** From mid-2025 to early 2026 the Perth electricity series swung wildly, with annual changes between −29% and +289%. That looks like government bill credits switching on and off, though that's my reading and the data doesn't say so. If you had a credit that has ended, your bill would jump even though tariffs barely moved.\n- **Usage:** The CPI measures price, not how much you use. Heating or cooling, a new appliance, an EV or more people at home can all raise a bill without a price rise.\n- **Other costs:** Overall prices in Perth are up 4.3% on a year ago, so the rest of your budget may be adding to the feeling.\n- **Your own tariff:** Check your usage and the billing period length. A longer billing period will also make a bill look bigger.\n\nIf you give me your bill's usage in kWh and billing period, I can help work out whether it looks high. I can also pull the Perth electricity index to show the cumulative rise over the past few years.",
